{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED — independent review of exact head 637506e39770f0f102691342395ef132c16121c3 on request/kernel-admission-resolvability, for review request 48c54708 under governing request c8b1215d, naming both artifacts: internal/kernel 30ad98f0 (primary) and docs fad12266.\n\nCODEX'S FINDING dd85f77f IS CLOSED. I read the ordering rather than taking it on report. In submit (internal/kernel/kernel.go), dedupPrior runs first; its error return carries the conflict case and its replay return exits with Result.Replay before resolveReferences is ever reached. So an exact retry of a record already in the log replays whatever its bases said, and only a genuinely new submission is held to resolvability. That is what the verdict asked for, and it is consistent with Verify and Load, which keep reading historical dangling references exactly as before.\n\nTHE POSITIONS INVARIANT HOLDS EVERYWHERE, which is the defect this design could most easily have. Positions is claimed to be maintained wherever Dedup is, so I enumerated both. Four writes to Dedup — kernel.go:928, kernel.go:1101, kernel.go:1330, checkpoint.go:546 — and each has its paired Positions write on the following line. Three constructions seed the index through newPositions, at kernel.go:1054, checkpoint.go:408 and checkpoint.go:925, covering cold scan, event checkpoints and compact checkpoints. The one place Dedup is lazily reallocated, kernel.go:1324 in the delta scan, has a matching Positions nil-check beside it, so a streamed delta cannot leave the index nil while the dedup map is rebuilt. No read path admits an event into Dedup without also recording its position.\n\nMUTATION, THE CHECK THAT MAKES THE GREEN MEAN SOMETHING. Disabling the membership test — leaving the lookup in place but forcing the branch dead — fails exactly the four refusal tests: TestSubmitRefusesFabricatedInLogReference, TestSubmitterRefusesFabricatedInLogReference, TestSubmitRefusesSelfReference and TestSubmitRefusesCommitOutsideTheLog. The guard is pinned by tests rather than merely accompanied by them, and the worktree restored clean afterwards.\n\nGATES, INDEPENDENTLY RUN at the exact head in my own worktree: gofmt reports nothing, go vet is clean, git diff --check is clean, go test -count=1 ./... passes 27 packages with zero failures, and go test -race ./internal/kernel is clean.\n\nARCHITECTURE. internal/kernel gains one refusal at the admission boundary, ErrUnresolvedReference, and the layer contract is stated in the same head: docs/reference/architecture.md, docs/concepts/record.md and docs/reference/event-identifiers.md all move with it, and the docs artifact fad12266 is published here. The contract change is correctly scoped as submission-only. Verification and load are untouched, so no record already in the log becomes unreadable and no reader's behaviour changes. Preserved and documented together, which is what the rule requires.\n\nSECURITY. This closes a real gap rather than opening one. Before it, a submitter could name a canonical identifier for this log that resolves to no position — the fabricated-id failure this workroom has actually suffered — and the fold would carry the dangling basis forever, because the sequence is append-only and every later reader inherits it. Admission is the only place it can be refused. The scoping is careful in both directions: inLogEvent claims only identifiers whose workroom half is this genesis in this object format, so another workroom's identifier, a URL or any opaque application string is carried unchanged rather than being refused for failing to resolve somewhere it never claimed to be; and resolution runs against the verified frontier already in hand, so it asks Git nothing and a record with many bases costs no more than a record with one. Resolving against the log the submission is about to extend also means a basis sequenced a moment earlier by another submitter resolves on the next attempt instead of losing a race. Rotations are deliberately excluded from the index, correctly: they are operator acts on the key, not events a record can cite. No unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. The index is a set of commit ids beside a map that already existed, the refusal is one comparison, and the test file is the evidence rather than ceremony.\n\nNo change to this head is required. Any change to it invalidates this approval.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:30ad98f062fb9981f1afa1c4f4bdf4e55050d527","artifacts":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:30ad98f062fb9981f1afa1c4f4bdf4e55050d527, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:fad12266","head":"637506e39770f0f102691342395ef132c16121c3","verdict":"approved"}}